\section{Introduction to Multi-Stage Programming}\label{sec:msp-intro}

Programming languages have long offered mechanisms for generating code at run time: \texttt{eval} functions, macro systems, template strings, and reflection facilities. These mechanisms are powerful but dangerous---they operate outside the type system, evade static analysis, and can introduce subtle bugs that manifest only when generated code is executed. \emph{Multi-stage programming} (MSP) offers a principled alternative: a type-safe framework for writing programs that construct and execute other programs, with clear boundaries between computation stages.

The central idea is deceptively simple. We annotate a program with \emph{staging annotations} that distinguish computations performed at different times. Code that runs at \emph{compile time} (or \emph{generation time}) constructs fragments of code that will run at \emph{run time} (or \emph{execution time}). The type system guarantees that generated code is well-formed and well-typed before it ever executes.

Multi-stage programming traces its intellectual ancestry to several sources: the Lisp macro tradition, which demonstrated the power of code-as-data; the $\lambda$-calculus with modal operators explored by Davies and Pfenning~\cite{davies2001modal}; and the practical need for generating specialized, high-performance code from generic, maintainable programs. The convergence of these threads produced languages like MetaML~\cite{taha1997metaml}, MetaOCaml~\cite{kiselyov2004metaml}, and the Lightweight Modular Staging (LMS) framework~\cite{rompf2010lms}.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of multi-stage programming from the ground up. We begin with the syntax and intuition of staging annotations, develop the modal type theory that underpins them, examine the key implementations, and conclude with applications---most notably, the generation of efficient code from interpreters.

\section{Staging Annotations: Code Quotation and Splicing}\label{sec:staging-annotations}

\subsection{The Basic Idea}

Consider a simple power function:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{pow}(n, x) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } n = 0 \\ x \cdot \mathit{pow}(n-1, x) & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
\end{equation}

When we call $\mathit{pow}(3, x)$, we perform three multiplications at run time. But if we know $n = 3$ at compile time, we could generate specialized code:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{pow3}(x) = x \cdot x \cdot x
\end{equation}

This eliminates the loop overhead and the recursive calls. Multi-stage programming makes this specialization \emph{systematic} and \emph{type-safe}.

\subsection{Brackets, Escapes, and Runs}

The three fundamental staging annotations are:

\begin{definition}[Staging Annotations]\label{def:staging-annotations}
Given a multi-stage language with at least two stages, the following constructs control staging:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Code quotation} (brackets): $\langle e \rangle$ delays the evaluation of $e$, producing a code value representing $e$.
  \item \textbf{Code splicing} (escapes): $\sim e$ evaluates $e$ in the current (earlier) stage and inserts the result into the enclosing quotation.
  \item \textbf{Code execution} (run): $\mathbf{run}\;e$ evaluates a code value $e$ to produce its result in the current stage.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Using these annotations, we can write a staged power function:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{spow}(n, x) = \mathbf{case}\;n\;\mathbf{of}\;\begin{cases} 0 \Rightarrow \langle 1 \rangle \\ n' \Rightarrow \langle x \cdot \sim(\mathit{spow}(n', x)) \rangle \end{cases}
\end{equation}

Calling $\mathit{spow}(3, \langle y \rangle)$ produces the code value $\langle y \cdot (y \cdot (y \cdot 1)) \rangle$. Applying $\mathbf{run}$ to this value yields the result of evaluating $y^3$.

\subsection{The Type of Code Values}

A code value of type $\langle \tau \rangle$ (sometimes written $\mathbf{code}\;\tau$ or $\bigcirc \tau$) represents a fragment of code that, when executed, produces a value of type $\tau$. The brackets constructor and the run operator are dual:

\begin{align}
\langle - \rangle &: \tau \to \langle \tau \rangle \label{eq:bracket-type}\\
\mathbf{run} &: \langle \tau \rangle \to \tau \label{eq:run-type}
\end{align}

In a well-typed program, the type system tracks which stage each expression belongs to, preventing the accidental mixing of values from different stages.

\subsection{Cross-Stage Persistence}

When a variable from an earlier stage is used inside a quotation, it must be \emph{lifted} into the code value. This is called \emph{cross-stage persistence}. In MetaOCaml, this is handled implicitly for values of base types; in MetaML, explicit annotations are required.

\begin{example}[Cross-Stage Persistence]\label{ex:cross-stage}
Consider:
\begin{verbatim}
  let n = 5 in
  < fun x -> ~n + x >
\end{verbatim}
The variable $n$ is bound in stage~0 but used inside a stage~1 quotation. The staging system must ensure that $n$'s value (5) is embedded into the generated code. The result is a code value equivalent to $\langle \mathit{fun}\;x \to 5 + x \rangle$.
\end{example}

\section{Temporal Type Theory}\label{sec:temporal-type-theory}

\subsection{Motivation: Why Types for Staging?}

Without type discipline, staging annotations are a source of errors. A splice $\sim e$ might appear outside any quotation, or a variable might be used at a stage where it is not bound. The type system must prevent these errors statically.

Davies and Pfenning~\cite{davies2001modal} showed that the appropriate type-theoretic foundation is \emph{modal logic}, specifically the intuitionistic modal logic $\mathbf{S4}$. The code type $\langle \tau \rangle$ corresponds to the modal necessity operator $\Box \tau$: a value of type $\Box \tau$ is a proof of $\tau$ that is valid at all accessible worlds---or, in the computational reading, a code fragment that can be executed in any future stage.

\subsection{The Modal Lambda Calculus}\label{subsec:modal-lambda}

Davies and Pfenning's $\lambda^{\Box}$ calculus extends the simply-typed lambda calculus with a modal type constructor $\Box$ and two new term formers:

\begin{grammar}{Modal Lambda Calculus $\lambda^{\Box}$}
  \production{e}{x \mid \lambda x{:}\tau.\,e \mid e_1\;e_2 \mid \mathbf{box}\;e \mid \mathbf{letbox}\;u = e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2}
  \production{\tau}{\alpha \mid \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \mid \Box\tau}
\end{grammar}

The typing rules introduce two new judgments. The $\mathbf{box}$ constructor requires its argument to be typed in an \emph{empty} local context (but a valid global context):

\begin{typerules}{Modal Lambda Calculus}
  \typerule{Var}{x : \tau \in \Delta}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash x : \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{}{\Delta \mid \Gamma, x{:}\tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash \lambda x{:}\tau_1.\,e : \tau_1 \to \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \quad \Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_1}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash e_1\;e_2 : \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Box}{\Delta \mid \cdot \vdash e : \tau}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash \mathbf{box}\;e : \Box\tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{LetBox}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash e_1 : \Box\tau_1 \quad \Delta, u{:}\tau_1 \mid \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_2}{\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash \mathbf{letbox}\;u = e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2 : \tau_2}
\end{typerules}

The key rule is \textsc{Box}: to form a value of type $\Box \tau$, the expression $e$ must be well-typed using \emph{only} the global context $\Delta$---the local variables in $\Gamma$ are not available. This enforces the stage boundary: code that will run later cannot depend on variables that exist only now.

\subsection{The Two-Level Context}

The typing judgment $\Delta \mid \Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ uses a \emph{two-level context}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Delta$ (the \emph{global} or \emph{previous-stage} context) contains variables that are available at all stages---they have already been computed and their values are persistent.
  \item $\Gamma$ (the \emph{local} or \emph{current-stage} context) contains variables available only at the current stage.
\end{itemize}

When we enter a $\mathbf{box}$, the local context is discarded (set to $\cdot$), and the global context is extended with the bound variable when we enter a $\mathbf{letbox}$.

\begin{insight}
The two-level context is the type-theoretic embodiment of a simple principle: \emph{code that will run later can only depend on values that exist now or have been explicitly embedded into the code}. This is the same principle that governs macro hygiene and closure semantics, made precise by the type system.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Normalization and the $\beta\eta$-Equations}

The modal lambda calculus satisfies standard $\beta$ and $\eta$ equations, extended with modal variants:

\begin{align}
(\lambda x.\,e_1)\;e_2 &\equiv_\beta e_1[e_2/x] \\
\mathbf{letbox}\;u = \mathbf{box}\;e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2 &\equiv_{\beta_\Box} e_2[e_1/u] \\
\lambda x.\,e\;x &\equiv_\eta e \quad (\text{if } x \notin \mathrm{fv}(e)) \\
\mathbf{letbox}\;u = e\;\mathbf{in}\;\mathbf{box}\;u &\equiv_{\eta_\Box} e
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}[Strong Normalization]\label{thm:modal-sn}
Every well-typed term of $\lambda^{\Box}$ is strongly normalizing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The proof proceeds by a computability argument analogous to the proof for the simply-typed lambda calculus (Theorem~\ref{thm:stlc-sn}). One defines a logical relation indexed by types and shows that every well-typed term is computable. The modal cases require extending the relation to account for the two-level context structure. The key insight is that $\mathbf{box}$ and $\mathbf{letbox}$ do not introduce new computational effects---they merely restructure the context---so normalization is preserved.
\end{proof}

\section{Modal Types for Staging}\label{sec:modal-types}

\subsection{From $\lambda^{\Box}$ to a Staging Language}

The modal lambda calculus provides the type-theoretic foundation, but practical staging languages add several features:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Multiple stages}: Rather than two levels, practical systems support an unbounded number of stages, with annotations that increment or decrement the current stage.
  \item \textbf{Code inspection}: Some systems allow pattern matching on code values, enabling \emph{open code} manipulation.
  \item \textbf{Cross-stage persistence}: Automatic lifting of values from earlier stages into code quotations.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic generation}: The $\mathbf{run}$ operator, which executes a code value to produce a run-time result.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Modal Type System for $N$ Stages}

We can generalize the two-level context to an $N$-level context. At stage $k$, the context is a sequence $\Gamma_0, \Gamma_1, \ldots, \Gamma_k$ where $\Gamma_i$ contains the variables bound at stage $i$. The code type constructor $\langle \tau \rangle$ (or $\bigcirc \tau$) increments the stage:

\begin{definition}[Multi-Stage Typing]\label{def:multi-stage-typing}
Let $\vec{\Gamma} = \Gamma_0, \Gamma_1, \ldots, \Gamma_n$ be a multi-level context. The typing judgment $\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k e : \tau$ means that $e$ is well-typed at stage $k$ in context $\vec{\Gamma}$. The rules include:

\begin{align}
&\frac{x : \tau \in \Gamma_i \quad i \leq k}{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k x : \tau} \textsc{Var} \\[8pt]
&\frac{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_{k+1} e : \tau}{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k \langle e \rangle : \langle \tau \rangle} \textsc{Bracket} \\[8pt]
&\frac{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k e : \langle \tau \rangle}{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_{k+1} \sim e : \tau} \textsc{Escape} \\[8pt]
&\frac{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k e : \langle \tau \rangle}{\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k \mathbf{run}\;e : \tau} \textsc{Run}
\end{align}
\end{definition}

The \textsc{Bracket} rule moves one stage deeper; the \textsc{Escape} rule moves one stage shallower. The \textsc{Run} rule stays at the same stage but converts a code value to its underlying type.

\subsection{Well-Scopedness and Well-Scoped Code}

A critical property of the modal type system is that it ensures \emph{well-scopedness} of generated code: every free variable in a code fragment is either bound within the fragment or is a persistent variable from an earlier stage. This rules out the generation of code with dangling references.

\begin{definition}[Well-Scoped Code]\label{def:well-scoped-code}
A code value $\langle e \rangle$ is \emph{well-scoped} if every free variable of $e$ is either:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Bound by a $\lambda$-abstraction within $e$, or
  \item Bound in $\Gamma_i$ for some $i < k$ (a persistent variable from an earlier stage).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Well-Scopedness]\label{thm:well-scoped}
If $\vec{\Gamma} \vdash_k \langle e \rangle : \langle \tau \rangle$, then $e$ is well-scoped.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the typing derivation. The \textsc{Bracket} rule ensures that the body $e$ is typed at stage $k+1$, where only variables from $\Gamma_0, \ldots, \Gamma_k$ (the persistent context) and variables bound within $e$ are available. Variables from $\Gamma_{k+1}$ (if any existed) would not be accessible, but the bracket rule starts with the context as-is, so no new local variables are introduced.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The Problem of Unrestricted Escape}

A subtlety arises with the interaction of escapes and let-bindings. Consider:

\begin{verbatim}
  let x = ~<5> in x + 1
\end{verbatim}

Here, $\sim \langle 5 \rangle$ tries to escape from stage 0 to stage 1, but $x$ is bound at stage 0. If we later try to use $x$ inside a quotation, we face a problem: $x$ was computed by running code from a deeper stage, and its relationship to the staging structure is unclear.

This is the \emph{variable-escape} problem, and it led to the development of \emph{closed code} types in MetaOCaml, where the type system distinguishes between code that may contain free variables (open code) and code that is self-contained (closed code).

\section{MetaML and MetaOCaml}\label{sec:metaml-metaocaml}

\subsection{MetaML: The First Multi-Stage Language}

MetaML, designed by Taha and Sheard~\cite{taha1997metaml}, was the first language to implement multi-stage programming with a sound type system. Built as an extension of Standard ML, MetaML introduced the keywords \texttt{<{}>}, \texttt{\~{}}, and \texttt{run} for staging annotations.

\begin{example}[MetaML Power Function]\label{ex:metaml-power}
\begin{verbatim}
  fun pow n =
    if n = 0 then <1>
    else < ~<fn x => x * ~(pow (n-1))> >
\end{verbatim}
Calling \texttt{pow 3} produces a code value representing the function $\lambda x.\,x \cdot (x \cdot (x \cdot 1))$. Applying \texttt{run} to this code and then to an argument computes the cube.
\end{example}

MetaML's type system was based on a variant of the modal lambda calculus, extended with ML-style polymorphism. The system guaranteed that generated code was well-typed, but it had limitations: cross-stage persistence required explicit annotations, and the system did not support \emph{open code} (code with free variables).

\subsection{MetaOCaml: Refinements and Open Code}

MetaOCaml~\cite{kiselyov2004metaml,kiselyov2014metaocaml}, developed by Kiselyov and collaborators, refined MetaML's design in several important ways:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Open code types}: The type $\langle \tau \rangle_{\mathit{open}}$ allows free variables, while $\langle \tau \rangle_{\mathit{closed}}$ requires code to be self-contained. This distinction is crucial for building code compositionally.
  \item \textbf{Persistent values}: Cross-stage persistence is handled automatically for pure values, with explicit \texttt{cpersist} annotations for effectful computations.
  \item \textbf{Name-based hygienic splicing}: Splicing respects lexical scoping, preventing accidental variable capture.
  \item \textbf{Efficient code representation}: Code values are represented as typed abstract syntax trees, with sharing and efficient substitution.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}[MetaOCaml Staged Interpreter]\label{ex:metaocaml-interpreter}
\begin{verbatim}
  type exp =
    | Var of string
    | Lam of string * exp
    | App of exp * exp
    | Int of int
    | Add of exp * exp

  let rec interp env = function
    | Var x -> env x
    | Lam (x, body) -> fun v -> interp (fun y -> if y = x then v else env y) body
    | App (f, x) -> (interp env f) (interp env x)
    | Int n -> n
    | Add (e1, e2) -> (interp env e1) + (interp env e2)
\end{verbatim}
To stage this interpreter, we change the return type to produce code values:
\begin{verbatim}
  let rec staged_interp env = function
    | Var x -> env x
    | Lam (x, body) -> .<fun v -> .~(staged_interp (fun y ->
        if y = x then .<v>. else env y) body)>.
    | App (f, x) -> .<.~(staged_interp env f) .~(staged_interp env x)>.
    | Int n -> .<n>.
    | Add (e1, e2) -> .<.~(staged_interp env e1) + .~(staged_interp env e2)>.
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

