\section{Lightweight Modular Staging (LMS)}\label{sec:lms}

\subsection{Design Philosophy}

Lightweight Modular Staging (LMS), developed by Rompf and Odersky~\cite{rompf2010lms,rompf2012lms}, takes a radically different approach to multi-stage programming. Rather than extending the language with new syntactic constructs (brackets, escapes, runs), LMS uses the \emph{type system} to distinguish stages.

The key insight is that a \emph{type} can carry staging information. In LMS (implemented as a Scala framework), values of type \texttt{T} are computed at the current stage, while values of type \texttt{Rep[T]} are \emph{representations}---code fragments that will produce values of type \texttt{T} at the next stage.

\subsection{The Type-Based Staging Mechanism}

\begin{definition}[LMS Staging]\label{def:lms-staging}
In LMS, staging is controlled by the \texttt{Rep} type constructor:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A value of type \texttt{T} is a \emph{static} value, known at generation time.
  \item A value of type \texttt{Rep[T]} is a \emph{dynamic} value, represented as a computation graph node. Operations on \texttt{Rep} values build the graph rather than computing results.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[LMS Staged Power]\label{ex:lms-power}
\begin{verbatim}
  def power(n: Int, x: Rep[Double]): Rep[Double] =
    if (n == 0) 1.0
    else x * power(n - 1, x)
\end{verbatim}
Here, \texttt{n} has type \texttt{Int} (static), and \texttt{x} has type \texttt{Rep[Double]} (dynamic). The recursion on \texttt{n} happens at generation time, unrolling the loop. Operations on \texttt{x} build a computation graph. The result is a straight-line program that computes $x^n$ without any loop or recursion.
\end{example}

\subsection{The IR and Delta Processing}

LMS builds an intermediate representation (IR) as a \emph{sea of nodes}---a graph where each node represents a computation, and edges represent data dependencies. The IR supports several key features:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Effect tracking}: Each node is annotated with its side effects (pure, read, write, allocate). This enables aggressive reordering of pure computations.
  \item \textbf{Common subexpression elimination}: Identical computations are automatically shared.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination}: Nodes whose results are never used are removed.
  \item \textbf{Delta processing}: Transformations are expressed as \emph{rewrite rules} that pattern-match on the IR and produce optimized versions.
\end{enumerate}

